          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A
            %strong duploid
            is a
            = succeed "-like" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structure"} structure
            where
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/objects"} objects
            have a specific
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/polarity+in+type+theory"} polarity
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/composition"} composition
            is not necessarily
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associative"} associative
          %p
            Duploids are models of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/programming+languages"} programming languages
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/side+effects"} side effects
            and explicitly polarized
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/positive+type"} positive
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/negative+type"} negative
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/types"} types
            %span.newWikiWord
              call-by-value
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/call-by-value"} ?
            and
            %span.newWikiWord
              call-by-name
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/call-by-name"} ?
            programming paradigms. Duploids axiomatize the algebraic structure of effectful morphisms, and to a certain extent “pure” morphisms (those with no side effects) can be characterized by equational properties as thunkable and linear morphisms. Contrast with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/call-by-push-value"} call-by-push-value
            which directly provides a syntax for the pure morphisms (as values and stacks).
          %p
            A duploid can be constructed from an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjunction"} adjunction
            and the category of duploids is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reflective+subcategory"} coreflective
            in the category of adjunctions, equivalent to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subcategory"} subcategory
            of adjunctions satisfying an equalizing requirement. The intuition is that a duploid is what is left of an adjunction if we only consider (co)Kleisli morphisms and duploids can be identified with adjunctions whose categories can be recovered from the Kleisli morphisms.
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            A
            %strong pre-duploid
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒟
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{D}
            consists of
          %ol
            %li
              a set
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi 𝒟
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} |\mathcal{D}|
              of objects with a polarity map
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ϖ
                      %mo :
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mi 𝒟
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mo →
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                      %mo ,
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \varpi : |\mathcal{D}| \to \{+,-\}
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ϖ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \varpi(A) = +
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              is positive and otherwise negative.
            %li
              for every pair
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ,
                    %mi B
                    %mo ∈
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi 𝒟
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A,B \in |\mathcal{D}|
              a set of morphisms
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒟
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi A
                      %mo ,
                      %mi B
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{D}(A,B)
            %li
              for every compatible pair of morphisms
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi 𝒟
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo ,
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %mi g
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi 𝒟
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi B
                    %mo ,
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \in \mathcal{D}(A,B), g\in \mathcal{D}(B,C)
              a composite
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi g
                      %mo ⊙
                      %mi f
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi 𝒟
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi A
                      %mo ,
                      %mi C
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g \odot f \in \mathcal{D}(A,C)
            %li
              for every object
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              an morphism
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi id
                      %mi A
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi 𝒟
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo ,
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} id_A \in \mathcal{D}(A,A)
              that is the identity with respect to composition.
            %li
              For every
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi 𝒟
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi A
                      %mo ,
                      %mi B
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ,
                      %mi g
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi 𝒟
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi B
                      %mo ,
                      %mi C
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ,
                      %mi h
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi 𝒟
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi C
                      %mo ,
                      %mi D
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \in \mathcal{D}(A,B), g \in \mathcal{D}(B,C), h \in \mathcal{D}(C,D)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi h
                    %mo ⊙
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi g
                    %mo ⊙
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi h
                    %mo ⊙
                    %mi g
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⊙
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h \odot (g \odot f) = (h \odot g) \odot f
              when
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
              is negative or
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              is positive.
          %p
            Note that when restricted to positive objects or negative objects, composition and identities form a category, written
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒫
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{P}
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒩
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{N}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Q
                  %mo ,
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P,Q,R
            and negative objects as
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                    %mo ,
                    %mi M
                    %mo ,
                    %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L,M,N
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                  %mo •
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g \bullet f
            and when the middle object is negative we call it “negative composition” or “by-name composition” and notate it as
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi g
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g \circ f
          %ol
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo •
                    %mo •
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bullet\bullet
              \:
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo •
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi g
                    %mo •
                    %mi h
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi f
                    %mo •
                    %mi g
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo •
                    %mi h
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \bullet (g \bullet h)  = (f \bullet g) \bullet h
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∘
                    %mo ∘
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \circ\circ
              \:
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo ∘
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi g
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi h
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi f
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi g
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi h
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \circ (g \circ h)  = (f \circ g) \circ h
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo •
                    %mo ∘
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bullet\circ
              \:
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo •
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi g
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi h
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi f
                    %mo •
                    %mi g
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi h
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \bullet (g \circ h)  = (f \bullet g) \circ h
          %p
            and we can see that the only obstacle to associativity is that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo ∘
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi g
                  %mo •
                  %mi h
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \circ (g \bullet h)
            is not necessarily equal to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi f
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi g
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo •
                    %mi h
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (f \circ g) \bullet h
          %p
            A
            %strong duploid
            is a pre-duploid
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒟
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{D}
            plus two
            %strong polarity shifts
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⇓
                    %mo :
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi 𝒩
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mo →
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi 𝒫
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi and
                    %mo ⇑
                    %mo :
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi 𝒫
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mo →
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi 𝒩
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Downarrow : |\mathcal{N}| \to |\mathcal{P}| and \Uparrow : |\mathcal{P}| \to |\mathcal{N}|
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                    %mo ∈
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi 𝒫
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mo ,
                    %mi N
                    %mo ∈
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi 𝒩
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P \in |\mathcal{P}|, N \in |\mathcal{N}|
          %ol
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi delay
                      %mi P
                    %mo :
                    %mi P
                    %mo →
                    %mo ⇑
                    %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} delay_P : P \to \Uparrow P
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi force
                      %mi P
                    %mo :
                    %mo ⇑
                    %mi P
                    %mo →
                    %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} force_P : \Uparrow P \to P
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi wrap
                      %mi N
                    %mo :
                    %mi N
                    %mo →
                    %mo ⇓
                    %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} wrap_N : N \to \Downarrow N
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi unwrap
                      %mi N
                    %mo :
                    %mo ⇓
                    %mi N
                    %mo →
                    %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} unwrap_N : \Downarrow N \to N
          %p such that
          %ol
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∀
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi P
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi force
                      %mi P
                    %mo ∘
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi delay
                      %mi P
                    %mo •
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \forall f : A \to P, force_P \circ (delay_P \bullet f) = f
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∀
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi N
                    %mo →
                    %mi A
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi f
                    %mo ∘
                    %msub
                      %mi unwrap
                      %mi N
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo •
                    %msub
                      %mi wrap
                      %mi N
                    %mo =
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \forall f : N \to A, (f \circ unwrap_N) \bullet wrap_N = f
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi delay
                      %mi P
                    %mo •
                    %msub
                      %mi force
                      %mi P
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi id
                      %mrow
                        %mo ⇑
                        %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} delay_P \bullet force_P = id_{\Uparrow P}
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi wrap
                      %mi N
                    %mo ∘
                    %msub
                      %mi unwrap
                      %mi N
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi id
                      %mrow
                        %mo ⇓
                        %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} wrap_N \circ unwrap_N = id_{\Downarrow N}
          %p In light of the definition of linear and thunkable morphisms, these identities are equivalent to saying
          %ol
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi wrap
                      %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} wrap_N
              is thunkable.
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi wrap
                      %mi N
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi unwrap
                      %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} wrap_N, unwrap_N
              is an isomorphism.
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi force
                      %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} force_P
              is linear.
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi force
                      %mi P
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi thunk
                      %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} force_P, thunk_P
              is an isomorphism.
          %h2#linear_and_thunkable_morphisms Linear and Thunkable Morphisms
          %p
            Just as thunkable morphisms can be defined in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/thunk-force+category"} thunk-force category
            and linear morphisms can be defined in a category with a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/runnable+monad"} runnable monad
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi delay
                    %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} delay_P
            in place of the thunk and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi unwrap
                    %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} unwrap_N
            in place of the run morphism. However, thunkable and linear morphisms can also be defined in a
            = succeed "," do
              %em pre-duploid
          %p
            A morphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            is
            %strong thunkable
            if for all compatible
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi g
                    %mo ,
                    %mi h
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g,h
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi h
                  %mo ⊙
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi g
                  %mo ⊙
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi h
                  %mo ⊙
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⊙
                  %mi f
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h \odot (g \odot f) = (h \odot g) \odot f.
          %p
            and a morphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            is
            %strong linear
            if for all compatible
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi g
                    %mo ,
                    %mi h
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g,h
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo ⊙
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi g
                  %mo ⊙
                  %mi h
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi f
                  %mo ⊙
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⊙
                  %mi h
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \odot (g \odot h) = (f \odot g) \odot h.
          %p
            Observe that all
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi P
                  %mo →
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f : P \to B
            are trivially linear and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g : A \to N
            are trivially thunkable. Further thunkable and linear morphisms form (non-full) subcategories
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒟
                      %mi t
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒟
                      %mi l
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{D}_t, \mathcal{D}_l
          %p
            To understand these concepts, consider the non-trivial situation where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f : A \to P
            is thunkable:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi h
                  %mo ∘
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi g
                  %mo •
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi h
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo •
                  %mi f
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h \circ (g \bullet f) = (h \circ g) \bullet f.
          %p
            on the left side, since we have a by-name composition,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi h
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h
            is “evaluated first”, whereas on the right side we have a by-value composition, so
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            is evaluated first. Thus these two morphisms being equal implies in many semantics
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            cannot perform any
            = succeed "." do
              %span.newWikiWord
                side-effect
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/side-effect"} ?
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            prints to the screen and so does
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi h
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            can manipulate its
            %span.newWikiWord
              continuation
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/continuation"} ?
            explicitly, it has to treat it linearly otherwise
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi h
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h
            may be evaluated twice or not at all.
          %p
            In the dual, if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi N
                  %mo →
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f : N \to B
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            is linear:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo ∘
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi g
                  %mo •
                  %mi h
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi f
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo •
                  %mi h
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \circ (g \bullet h) = (f \circ g) \bullet h
          %p
            holds and similarly
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            is evaluated first in the left morphism and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi h
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h
            is evaluated first in the right. Then as above it cannot perform any effects and has to treat its
            %em input
            linearly, as duplicating or dropping
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi h
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h
            would make the equation fail to hold.
          %h2#relation_to_adjunctions Relation to Adjunctions
          %p
            Briefly, the category of duploids and duploid functors is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reflective+subcategory"} reflective subcategory
            of the category of adjunctions and morphisms of adjunctions.
          %h3#a_duploid_from_an_adjunction A Duploid from an Adjunction
          %p
            First, we construct a duploid from an adjunction, which we think of as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/forgetful+functor"} forgetful functor
            that only remembers the Kleisli morphisms of the adjunction. To keep the proof unbiased, we start with a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/profunctor"} profunctor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi O
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mo −
                  %mi ⇸
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mo +
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} O : C_- &amp;#8696; C_+
            that the adjunction
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representable+functor"} represents
            = succeed "morphism" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/heteromorphism"} hetero-
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mo ∈
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mo +
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P \in C_+
            to a negative object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                  %mo ∈
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mo −
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N \in C_-
            as
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi O
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi P
                  %mo ,
                  %mi N
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} O(P,N)
            and can be used to define the Kleisli and coKleisli categories. In programming applications these are the “possibly effectful” morphisms. Then let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo ⊣
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F \dashv G
            be adjoint functors representing
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi O
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} O
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mo −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi F
                  %mi P
                  %mo ,
                  %mi N
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≅
                  %mi O
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi P
                  %mo ,
                  %mi N
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≅
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mo +
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi P
                  %mo ,
                  %mi G
                  %mi N
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_-(F P, N) \cong O(P,N) \cong C_+(P, G N).
          %p
            The pre-duploid
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒟
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{D}
            has as objects
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi 𝒟
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mo +
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mo +
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mo −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} |\mathcal{D}| = |C_+| + |C_-|
            with
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϖ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ϖ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi N
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \varpi(P) = +, \varpi(N) = -
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒟
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo ,
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi O
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi A
                    %mo +
                  %mo ,
                  %msup
                    %mi B
                    %mo −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{D}(A,B) = O(A^+,B^-)
          %p where
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi P
                    %mo +
                  %mo =
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P^+ = P
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi N
                    %mo +
                  %mo =
                  %mi G
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N^+ = G N
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi P
                    %mo −
                  %mo =
                  %mi F
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P^- = F P
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi N
                    %mo −
                  %mo =
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N^- = N
          %p and identities are given by the identities/unit and counit of the adjunction:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} id
                    %mi P
                  %mo :
                  %mi O
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi P
                  %mo ,
                  %mi F
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≡
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mo −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi F
                  %mi P
                  %mo ,
                  %mi F
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \id_P : O(P, F P) \equiv C_-(F P,F P)
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} id
                    %mi N
                  %mo :
                  %mi O
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi G
                  %mi N
                  %mo ,
                  %mi N
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≡
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mo +
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi G
                  %mi N
                  %mo ,
                  %mi G
                  %mi N
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \id_N : O(G N, N) \equiv C_+(G N,G N)
          %p
            To define composition of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi 𝒟
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo ,
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %mi g
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi 𝒟
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi B
                    %mo ,
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \in \mathcal{D}(A,B), g \in \mathcal{D}(B,C)
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                  %mo =
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B = P
            is positive, composition is interpreted as composition in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi C
                      %mo −
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kleisli+category"} Kleisli category
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi GF
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} GF
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi O
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi A
                    %mo +
                  %mo ,
                  %mi F
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≅
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mo −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi F
                  %msup
                    %mi A
                    %mo +
                  %mo ,
                  %mi F
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \in O(A^+,F P) \cong C_-(F A^+, F P)
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi O
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi P
                  %mo ,
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mo −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≅
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mo −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi F
                  %mi P
                  %mo ,
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mo −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g \in O(P, C^-) \cong C_-(F P, C^-)
          %p
            and similarly if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                  %mo =
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B = N
            is negative, composition is interpreted as composition in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi C
                      %mo +
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_+
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kleisli+category"} Kleisli category
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi FG
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} FG
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi O
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi A
                    %mo +
                  %mo ,
                  %mi N
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≅
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mo +
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi A
                    %mo +
                  %mo ,
                  %mi G
                  %mi N
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \in O(A^+,N) \cong C_+(A^+, G N)
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi O
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi G
                  %mi N
                  %mo ,
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mo −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≅
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mo +
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi G
                  %mi N
                  %mo ,
                  %mi G
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mo −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g \in O(G N, C^-) \cong C_+(G N, G C^-)
          %p
            This definition makes it clear that the identities are identity and the
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo •
                  %mo •
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bullet\bullet
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∘
                  %mo ∘
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \circ\circ
            laws hold from identity and associativity of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi C
                      %mo +
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi C
                      %mo −
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_+,C_-
          %h3#adjunction_from_a_duploid Adjunction from a Duploid
          %p From a duploid, we can construct the “cofree” adjunction: i.e., a right adjoint to the forgetful functor just defined. Intuitively, we want to recover the homomorphisms just from the heteromorphisms. The maximal choice is to consider all thunkable morphisms between positive types and linear morphisms between negative types to be homomorphisms.
          %p
            Furthermore, this functor is fully faithful, making the category of duploids a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reflective+subcategory"} reflective subcategory
            of adjunctions: they can be identified with exactly those adjunctions in which the unit and counit are mono and epi respectively and in which all thunkable heteromorphisms are the image under
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            of some homomorphism and vice-versa all linear heteromorphisms are in the image of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi η
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equalizer"} equalizer
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi GF
                  %mi η
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} GF\eta
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi η
                  %mi GF
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta GF
            and dually that the counit
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϵ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \epsilon
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coequalizer"} coequalizer
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi FG
                  %mi ϵ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} FG\epsilon
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϵ
                  %mi FG
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \epsilon FG
